window.docContents[88].resolve({"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L0-___-The-black-box--Risks-at-this-step":{"contents":"* Bare-real and dimension-only mistakes. Bare reals accept √(m/k) where √(k/m) is the angular-frequency formula, and dimension-only types accept angular frequency plus cyclic frequency because both have dimension T⁻¹. The kinded checks pin these risks at HCP.L0_BlackBox#bare_reals, HCP.L0_BlackBox#frequency_alias, HCP.L0_BlackBox#refused_kind_addition, and HCP.L0_BlackBox#refused_reciprocal_radicand.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L0 — The black box","header":"5.1.4. Risks at this step","id":"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L0-___-The-black-box--Risks-at-this-step"},"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L6-___-What-must-hold--Synopsis--Errors-prevented":{"contents":"* Unstatable scope: \"every pair\" or \"pair P\" written without a declared sort or a nominal object to quantify over (HCP.L6_Requirements#invalid_scopes).* Over-claimed coverage: a requirement about pair P counted as governing pair Q's output (HCP.L6_Requirements#scope_gap).* Ungoverned output: a model output that no requirement governs goes unreported (HCP.L6_Requirements#requirement_census).\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L6 — What must hold\u0009Synopsis","header":"5.7.1.4. Errors prevented","id":"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L6-___-What-must-hold--Synopsis--Errors-prevented"},"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Requirements--The-measurement-model___-licensed-distribution___-and-stated-requirements-_LPAR_R26___R28_RPAR_":{"contents":"R26 — A module's behavior is a checked measurement model. The metrological-modularity chapter's\nbehavior clause, stated as an expressiveness requirement: a declared boundary\ncarries relation edges (Provenance.Relation) whose witnesses are kernel-checked\ntheorems; every relation kind has a conclusion-shape check; a boundedBy edge names a\nkinded tolerance; named side conditions are checked against the witness statement; and\nevery carrier rung beyond the witness's own is answered for by a named repair or\nrestatement (RelationLicense) — a rung claimed with neither is refused. The checks are\nthe elaborator's (#kind_relation, surveyed by #kind_relations), pinned by acceptance\nand refusal probes for all four relation kinds.\n\nR27 — A module's distribution is licensed by declared extensivity. The mereology\nclause as a verifiable requirement: produced ports carry aggregation classes\n(Provenance.AggregationClass — the §13.5 vocabulary of the extensivity chapter,\nextended by the count-keyed, parameter-conditioned, and interface-licensed classes,\neach naming the tolerance, sortal, transport, or law that is the content of its\nclaim), and recarving or sharding a module's batch axis is licensed by theorem from\nthose declarations: Recarving.distribution_license — one map, every\ndeclared-extensive output's total preserved — with the quasi-extensive price at most\nboth carvings' joins times the per-join tolerance, tied to the rounding budget where\nthe carrier demands it. The clause's hygiene is the elaborator's\n(checkAggregations, rendered in every #kind_contract report), pinned by\nacceptance and refusal probes.\n\nR28 — A module's requirements are stated with their evidence. The requirement clause\nof the metrological-modularity chapter, stated as an\nexpressiveness requirement: every produced port of a model boundary is governed by a\nProvenance.Requirement or exempted with a reason (@[kindRequirementFree]); object\nscopes are compared with the result domain inferred from the member's return type, while\nmissing indices or unresolved object domains are unchecked violations unless exempted;\n.sort requires an indexed result and a Sorted instance; the census first reduces the\nactual result bearer's sort inside the member telescope, then falls back to object scopes\nor is ungoverned on a definite mismatch, while a stuck sort follows the object-domain path\nand may be unchecked. A .decided scope requires a deciderProof theorem proving that its\ndecider holds for every result of the governed member; otherwise the port is unchecked. A provable\nrequirement's witness is a sorry-free theorem, or a theorem edge to a specification\nboundary, whose statement names no @[kindReferent]-marked declaration, with Bool spot\nchecks the command evaluates where the model runs; an empirical requirement names marked\nreferents and a gate mentioning one, and no witness; a claimed absence is a license the\nenvironment holds no instance of; and an attestation never stands beside a discharge. The\nchecks are the elaborator's (#kind_requirement, surveyed by #kind_requirements,\ncensused by #kind_requirement_coverage and gated by #kind_requirement_clean), pinned\nby acceptance and refusal probes; what the clause buys is argued in\nevery claim a model makes, with its evidence.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Requirements","header":"2.13. The measurement model, licensed distribution, and stated requirements (R26–R28)","id":"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Requirements--The-measurement-model___-licensed-distribution___-and-stated-requirements-_LPAR_R26___R28_RPAR_"},"/#hcp-l2":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols","header":"5.3. L2 — The pair is a system too","id":"/#hcp-l2"}});